ranked.helper_design:
PLACEMENT AND SIGNATURE. Add a new module crates/store/src/tx.rs (`mod tx;` in lib.rs). Move finish_tx out of canonical.rs:1647 into it, and add an associated function on Db. That makes it 'Db-level' and keeps call sites in the existing `Self::assert_heads_match(&conn, ..)` style:

```rust
impl Db {
    /// BEGIN IMMEDIATE, run `body`, COMMIT. On ANY error from the body or the COMMIT: best-effort ROLLBACK and return the ORIGINAL error (issue 498).
    /// `body` must be a lazy future (an `async {}` block or an `async fn` call); it is first polled AFTER the BEGIN succeeds.
    pub(crate) async fn immediate<T>(conn: &Connection, body: impl Future<Output = turso::Result<T>>) -> turso::Result<T> {
        Self::within(conn, "BEGIN IMMEDIATE", body).await
    }
    /// The same with an explicit BEGIN form, so the two plain-`BEGIN` sites (canonical.rs:11352, lib.rs:4018) convert byte-identically.
    pub(crate) async fn within<T>(conn: &Connection, begin: &'static str, body: impl Future<Output = turso::Result<T>>) -> turso::Result<T> {
        conn.execute(begin, ()).await?; // nothing opened: no ROLLBACK, and never end a transaction this call did not open
        finish(conn, body.await).await
    }
}
pub(crate) async fn finish<T>(conn: &Connection, result: turso::Result<T>) -> turso::Result<T> {
    let err = match result {
        Ok(v) => match conn.execute("COMMIT", ()).await { Ok(_) => return Ok(v), Err(e) => e },
        Err(e) => e,
    };
    rollback_best_effort(conn).await;
    Err(err)
}
async fn rollback_best_effort(conn: &Connection) {
    // turso has sometimes already ended the transaction itself: RAISE(ROLLBACK), a poisoned transaction at COMMIT, a generic error on a single-row write or a read, or an I/O failure after op_auto_commit flipped auto_commit.
    if conn.is_autocommit().unwrap_or(false) { return; }
    let _ = conn.execute("ROLLBACK", ()).await; // its error never replaces the original
    if !conn.is_autocommit().unwrap_or(true) { eprintln!("[store] ROLLBACK left the shared writer inside its transaction (issue 498)"); }
}
```

Why take a future rather than a closure? It is exactly how sites are already written: `let result = async {..}.await; finish_tx(&conn, result)`. Futures are lazy, so BEGIN still runs first. The body can borrow `&conn`, `&mut stmts`, `&mut pending`, `resolver: &mut _` and `&mut report` without higher-ranked lifetime trouble, and those borrows end when immediate returns. The alternative is `F: AsyncFnOnce() -> turso::Result<T>`, which edition 2024 allows; it removes the 'lazy future' caveat at the cost of a novel idiom in this codebase. Box::pin the big bodies at the call site (apply_tenders, twin_repair, repair_placeholder). The helper is generic and holds the body inline, so a large body enlarges the caller's future, and CLAUDE.md records that this has already overflowed the stack once.

FIT WITH THE GUARD STYLE. The caller keeps `let conn = self.conn().await;` (the MutexGuard) and passes `&conn`; the guard derefs to &Connection. Nothing in the helper touches the mutex. Checkpoints, publish_cursor and counters stay outside the call, after Ok, exactly where they sit today.

OPTIONAL DEFENSE IN DEPTH (recommended, separate unit). This is the only fix for the panic and cancellation cases above. In Db::conn_for (lib.rs:1191), after taking the guard: `if !guard.is_autocommit().unwrap_or(true) { eprintln!(\"[store] writer handed over INSIDE a transaction (waiter {caller}); rolling it back (issue 498)\"); let _ = guard.execute(\"ROLLBACK\", ()).await; }`. It mirrors the reader pool's is_autocommit check at read.rs:150. Precondition: no code spans one transaction across two conn() acquisitions. Every BEGIN..COMMIT in the 61 sites sits inside one guard, and nested acquisition would deadlock on the tokio mutex anyway.

TESTS. Put them in tx.rs `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`. The tests are in-crate, so the private `db.conn()` is reachable. Use the `Db::open(\"/tmp/tender-db-tx498-<case>-{pid}.db\")` convention, removing -wal/-shm first. Do not call db.foreign_keys_enabled() while holding the guard, because it re-acquires the guard and deadlocks; query `PRAGMA foreign_keys` on the guard instead.
(1) A failing body. Run CREATE TABLE t498(x INTEGER PRIMARY KEY). First run a CONTROL without the helper: BEGIN IMMEDIATE, INSERT 1, INSERT 1 (Err), then assert `!conn.is_autocommit()`. This proves turso left the transaction open, so the test is not vacuous. Then ROLLBACK. Next, `Db::immediate(&conn, async { INSERT 1?; INSERT 1?; Ok(()) })`. Assert: the error is the UNIQUE error, `conn.is_autocommit()`, a fresh BEGIN IMMEDIATE + COMMIT succeeds, and COUNT(t498)==0. Add a variant whose body INSERTs, then returns `Err(turso::Error::Error(\"boom\".into()))`, and assert the returned error is exactly \"boom\".
(2) A failing COMMIT, using a deferred FK. A RAISE in a temp trigger fires at STATEMENT time, so it fails the body, not the COMMIT; it cannot be the COMMIT lever. Db::open sets foreign_keys=ON (lib.rs:98). Create `p498(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY)` and `c498(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, p INTEGER REFERENCES p498(id) DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED)`. turso 0.7.2 has no defer_foreign_keys pragma, but it does honour DEFERRABLE (schema.rs:4459-4468, fkeys.rs:884). At COMMIT, check_deferred_fk_on_commit fails with 'deferred foreign key constraint failed on commit' and deliberately leaves the transaction open (execute.rs op_auto_commit). CONTROL first: BEGIN, INSERT c498(1,42), assert COMMIT is_err and `!is_autocommit()`, then ROLLBACK. Then call immediate with the same INSERT. Assert: Err contains 'deferred foreign key', is_autocommit, and a following BEGIN IMMEDIATE + COMMIT succeeds. That last check also proves the ROLLBACK cleared the deferred-violation counter, or this COMMIT would fail again. Finally assert COUNT(c498)==0. If DEFERRABLE ever stops being enforced, the control's is_err assertion fails loudly.
(3) The engine already rolled back. Create a trigger `CREATE TRIGGER r498 BEFORE INSERT ON t WHEN NEW.x=2 BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ROLLBACK,'r498 refused'); END`. The body inserts 1, then 2. turso ends the transaction itself (ResolveType::Rollback arm), so the helper's ROLLBACK is skipped or fails. Assert the returned error is 'r498 refused', is_autocommit, and count 0.
(4) A failing BEGIN. Open a manual BEGIN IMMEDIATE, then call `immediate(&conn, async { ran = true; Ok(()) })`. Assert Err 'cannot start a transaction within a transaction', `!ran`, and `!conn.is_autocommit()`: the outer transaction is not the helper's to end. Then ROLLBACK.
(5) The success path returns the value, the rows are visible, and is_autocommit holds.
Run a single test with the gate's flags and package set: `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0 CARGO_PROFILE_TEST_DEBUG=0 cargo test -p model -p store -p ingest -p tender-db --features tender-db/server tx::`. Then run ops/check.sh, redirect the output to a file, and read GATE-EXIT.

MECHANICAL ADOPTION. Each conversion is byte-identical on the success path: the same statements run in the same order between the same BEGIN and COMMIT.
(a) Pure tail swaps (`BEGIN?; let r = X.await; match r {Ok→COMMIT?, Err→ROLLBACK}` becomes `Self::immediate(&conn, X).await`): record_notice lib.rs:1741, reclaim_notice lib.rs:2284, insert_plan 11241, clear_plan 10976, retire_tenders_chunked 29874, twin_repair_writes 30474 (Box::pin body), record_merge_verdicts 21991, record_identifier_verdicts 22106 (publish_cursor stays after Ok), delete_user accounts.rs:266, and the four finish_tx users 11598, 11651, 11991, 13589.
(b) Bare loops wrapped in an async block: build_plan_groups 12369, 12393 and 12561, link_step 13465, upsert_currency_rates rates.rs:67, merge_provisional_organizations_batch 16017 (publish_cursor after Ok), backfill_original_lang lib.rs:3969 (its explicit per-row ROLLBACK collapses into `?`), and insert_legacy_ojs_keys 11352 and backfill_org_name_norm lib.rs:4018 via `Self::within(&conn, \"BEGIN\", ..)`.
(c) Small restructure, still byte-identical. In apply_tenders 15421 the body becomes `for p in chunk { let (a, head) = self.apply_tender_tx(..)?; .. } applied.leaf_rows += pending.flush(&conn).await?; Self::assert_heads_match(&conn, &rewrote).await?; Ok((applied, rewrote))`; changed_any, total and the checkpoint stay outside. In resolve_mentions 14362, the error/break pattern becomes `?`, and the stamp call goes inside the body.
(d) Conditional BEGIN on dry_run: repair_nested 27762, repair_placeholder 28364, backfill_merged_window 16188, restore_dropped_satellites 18500. Build the body once, then `let r = if dry_run { body.await } else { Self::immediate(&conn, body).await };`.
(e) Leave hand-written, or convert with care, because they act on the COMMIT error specifically: reparse_notice lib.rs:2533 times and logs COMMIT. match_org_identifiers_r2 17081, fold_provisional_plan 25696 and similar do a best-effort publish_cursor on failure; this can move to an `Err(e) =>` arm after immediate. The already-safe hand-written sites (about 30) can follow for uniformity, but carry no risk.
